>> title: Beef Bourguignon, Instant Pot
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: beef, french, wine, braise, instant pot, pressure cooker
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> dish: beef-bourguignon
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> aka: boeuf bourguignon, beef burgundy, burgundy beef, instant pot beef bourguignon, pressure cooker boeuf bourguignon
>> pairs-with: baguette
>> servings: 6
>> time: 2 hr
>> slack: forgiving — a sealed pot boiled nothing away, so the fifteen minutes reducing with the lid off is where the sauce is decided
>> step.2: render the lardons 8 min
>> step.3: brown 12 min, in two batches
>> step.4: soften, deglaze the base clean
>> step.5: 35 min high pressure, natural release 15 min
>> step.6: glaze the garnish 10 min
>> step.7: fold in, reduce lid off 15 min

The sauce is the dish and a sealed pot cannot reduce anything, so the wine goes in halved and the reduction is handed back to the stove at the end. No flour on the beef either — on a sealed base it scorches, and the fifteen minutes with the lid off is what thickens this.

Render @bacon{6%oz}(170 g; cut in lardons) on sauté in an #Instant Pot{} for ~render{8%min}, then lift the lardons out and keep them for the end.

Brown @beef chuck{3%lb}(1.4 kg; cut in 2-in cubes), @kosher salt{2%tsp} in @&(~1)bacon fat{} for ~brown{12%min}, in two batches.

Soften @carrots{2%large}(sliced), @yellow onion{1%large}(sliced), @garlic{2%cloves} with @&(~1)browned beef{}, then pour in @red Burgundy{1 1/2%cup}(355 mL; or any dry red) and scrape the base until nothing is stuck to it.

Pressure cook @&(~1)pot{} with @beef stock{1%cup}(240 mL), lid on and valve sealed: ~come to pressure{15%min}, then ~pressure cook{35%min} on high and ~natural release{15%min}.

Glaze @pearl onions{18}(peeled), @cremini mushrooms{1%lb}(450 g; quartered) in @unsalted butter{3%Tbs} in a #skillet{} for ~glaze{10%min} — a separate pan, because the pot is full and these want a dry heat and room.

Fold @&(~1)glazed garnish{} and the reserved lardons into @&(~2)braise{}, then ~reduce{15%min} on sauté with the lid off, until the sauce naps a spoon rather than running off it.
